  "summary": "Intentionally overdeveloping film to increase its sensitivity and contrast, typically used in low-light conditions.",
  "description": "Creates high-contrast images with deep blacks and blown-out highlights, increased grain, and often reduced shadow detail; this occurs due to extending development time to compensate for underexposure. Commonly used in photojournalism, documentary photography, and cinema to create dramatic, gritty visuals, particularly associated with black-and-white film and certain color film stocks like Kodak Tri-X.",
